From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 06:52:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 133/190] Put the latch in the status line's D field

cd_dtest's two bits are sticky -- bit 0 the first time cd_read_block()
is entered, bit 1 the first time a block is handed over -- and neither
is ever cleared. The boot probe sets bit 0 on every boot, so "DX" has
been on the status line permanently and means only "the drive was
touched once, at some point". It cannot distinguish reads being tried
and failing from nothing being asked at all, which is the distinction
that mattered.

Pack the live dead-drive latch into bits 8 and 9 of CDREPORT[27].
---
 bios/segacd.c | 14 +++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index fdc1a1d..e010c8a 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -1873,7 +1873,19 @@ static void cdr_report(void)
     CDREPORT[24] = cd_seeks;
     CDREPORT[25] = cd_hits;
     CDREPORT[26] = cd_errors;
-    CDREPORT[27] = cd_dtest;
+    /* cd_dtest's two bits are sticky: bit 0 goes on the first time
+     * cd_read_block() is entered and bit 1 the first time a block is
+     * handed over, and neither is ever cleared. So "1, not 2" has been
+     * on the status line since the boot probe touched the drive, on
+     * every boot, and stays there whether or not anything has asked
+     * since. It cannot tell "reads are being tried and failing" from
+     * "nothing has been asked in ten minutes".
+     *
+     * The latch is what separates them, and unlike the other two it is
+     * a live value, re-read every report. */
+    CDREPORT[27] = (UWORD)(cd_dtest
+                         | (cd_dead ? 0x100 : 0)
+                         | (cd_deadrun ? 0x200 : 0));
     CDREPORT[28] = cd_why;
     /* The drive's own state, nibble 0 of its status packet. The
      * BIOS dispatches sixteen ways on this byte at $140E and this
